---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site-config';
import { SITES } from '../../lib/sites';
import { breadcrumbJsonLd, organizationJsonLd } from '../../lib/seo';

const origin = SITES.manufacturer;
const canonical = `${origin}/verification/`;

const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: `${origin}/` },
  { name: 'Verification', url: canonical },
];

const rows = [
  {
    name: 'Verified',
    cls: 'status-verified',
    meaning: 'A reliable source, or on-site / document verification by us, supports the field.',
    established: 'Independent source, or our own on-site inspection or document review.',
    presented: 'Stated as a supported fact, with the source noted.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Supplier Reported',
    cls: 'status-supplier',
    meaning: 'The value was provided by the supplier about themselves.',
    established: 'Supplier statement only (email, form, datasheet, website).',
    presented: 'Labelled as self-reported. Never stated as a verified fact.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Third-Party Verified',
    cls: 'status-third-party',
    meaning: 'Independent third-party testing or certification supports the field.',
    established: 'Accredited lab report or certification body evidence.',
    presented: 'Stated as third-party supported, with the evidence type noted.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Unverified',
    cls: 'status-unverified',
    meaning: 'The field has not yet been checked against any source.',
    established: 'No verification performed yet.',
    presented: 'Shown as unconfirmed and not relied upon.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = [
  organizationJsonLd({
    id: `${origin}/`,
    name: site.name,
    description: site.description,
    logo: `${origin}/logo.svg`,
  }),
  breadcrumbJsonLd(crumbs),
];
---

<BaseLayout
  site={site}
  title="Verification Model"
  description="How Charcoal Hub labels manufacturer data: Verified, Supplier Reported, Third-Party Verified and Unverified — and why 'Supplier Reported' is not a verified fact."
  canonical={canonical}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <div class="container">
    <div class="page-title">
      <h1>Verification model</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Every manufacturer profile field carries one of four verification statuses. Statuses
        describe <em>how well supported</em> a value is — not how good a supplier is. A single
        profile can hold fields at different levels, and each is labelled where it appears.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="grid grid-2">
        <div class="card">
          <h3><span class="badge status-verified">Verified</span></h3>
          <p>
            Supported by a reliable, independently checkable source — or confirmed through our own
            on-site inspection or document review. This is the strongest level we assign, and it is
            reserved for fields we can actually stand behind.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3><span class="badge status-supplier">Supplier Reported</span></h3>
          <p>
            The value was provided by the supplier themselves — by email, form, datasheet or their
            own website. It is published for context and traceability and is <strong>always
            labelled as self-reported</strong>. We do not present it as an established fact.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3><span class="badge status-third-party">Third-Party Verified</span></h3>
          <p>
            Supported by independent third-party evidence — for example an accredited laboratory
            test report or a certification body — rather than by the supplier’s or our own
            statement alone.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3><span class="badge status-unverified">Unverified</span></h3>
          <p>
            Not yet checked against any source. Treated as unconfirmed and never relied upon as a
            fact until the field moves to a stronger status.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>What each status means in practice</h2>
        <p>The table below is the reference for how a status is established and how the field is then presented.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Status</th>
              <th>What it means</th>
              <th>How it is established</th>
              <th>How we present it</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              rows.map((r) => (
                <tr>
                  <td><span class={`badge ${r.cls}`}>{r.name}</span></td>
                  <td>{r.meaning}</td>
                  <td>{r.established}</td>
                  <td>{r.presented}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Why “Supplier Reported” is not a verified fact</h2>
        <p>
          Self-reported data is useful: it tells a buyer what a supplier claims, and which claims
          are worth testing. It is also the easiest thing in cross-border sourcing to get wrong,
          because a plausible-looking number — a monthly tonnage, a certification code, an
          export-market list — reads like a fact the moment it is published without a label.
        </p>
        <p>We separate the two deliberately:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>A claim is not evidence.</strong> A supplier saying they produce
            <em>a given volume</em> is a statement about themselves. It only becomes “Verified”
            when a reliable source or our own inspection supports it, and only becomes
            “Third-Party Verified” when independent testing or certification does.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The label travels with the data.</strong> Wherever a self-reported value is
            shown, the “Supplier Reported” label is shown with it. It is never silently upgraded by
            proximity to verified fields.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Buyers can weight it themselves.</strong> A labelled claim lets a buyer decide
            how much to rely on it, and ask for evidence before committing — which is the point of
            the model.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <div class="callout">
          <span class="callout-title">Rule of thumb</span>
          <p>
            If we cannot say where a value came from, we do not publish it as fact. “Supplier
            Reported” is a label, not a shortcut around verification.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2>Unverified</h2>
        <p>
          “Unverified” is a working state, not a verdict. It means the field exists as a candidate
          data point but has not been checked. It is never presented as confirmed, and it is the
          first field a qualification pass will attempt to resolve.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="meta-line">
        Next: see <a href="/how-we-qualify/">how we qualify a supplier</a>, or return to the
        <a href="/">overview</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="cta-banner">
        <h2>Ask for data with its status attached</h2>
        <p>
          The Charcoal Hub sourcing desk will return manufacturer information with the verification
          status of each field included.
        </p>
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={`${SITES.main}/contact/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Request a Quote</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
